1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,007
Your CTO asks: "Security
reviewed commit X on Friday.

2
00:00:08,047 --> 00:00:12,893
Monday's build was made from commit
Y. The diff between X and Y is empty.

3
00:00:12,933 --> 00:00:15,302
Did we deploy reviewed code or not?"

4
00:00:15,642 --> 00:00:20,210
Two IDs. An empty diff between them,
so not one line of code differs.

5
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:24,151
Both facts are true at the same
time, and neither is a bug.

6
00:00:24,191 --> 00:00:26,088
How? Say your guess out loud.

7
00:00:30,928 --> 00:00:36,407
A commit ID is the hash of the whole commit
object, and that object contains the

8
00:00:36,447 --> 00:00:42,203
moment the commit was created. A commit is
one saved snapshot of the project, with a

9
00:00:42,243 --> 00:00:47,584
note about who saved it and when. A hash
is a fingerprint computed from bytes.

10
00:00:47,924 --> 00:00:52,904
An amend, a rebase or a cherry-pick, three
commands that later videos teach, writes

11
00:00:52,944 --> 00:00:56,170
a new object with the same
content and a different ID.

12
00:00:56,210 --> 00:01:00,947
To answer the CTO you need to know exactly
what is in a commit object, what git

13
00:01:00,987 --> 00:01:03,850
commit writes, and what
the ID is computed from.

14
00:01:05,690 --> 00:01:09,578
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:09,618 --> 00:01:14,858
You've met commits twice: as one of four
object types in video 8, and as the thing

16
00:01:14,898 --> 00:01:20,267
the index turns into in video 15. The next
three videos are about the commit itself.

17
00:01:20,607 --> 00:01:25,486
Hold on to one idea through all three: a
commit is a small text object that never

18
00:01:25,526 --> 00:01:30,526
changes after it is written. Every command
that seems to change one, amend, rebase,

19
00:01:30,566 --> 00:01:35,444
cherry-pick, sign, writes another object
and moves a ref, a name that points at a

20
00:01:35,484 --> 00:01:35,869
commit.

21
00:01:36,209 --> 00:01:41,093
Today: the fields of a commit, the six
steps of git commit, the files it touches

22
00:01:41,133 --> 00:01:46,264
under .git, and the commit ID computed by
hand. By the end, you'll answer the CTO in

23
00:01:46,304 --> 00:01:47,064
one sentence.

24
00:01:48,904 --> 00:01:50,482
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:50,822 --> 00:01:52,234
After this video you can:

26
00:01:52,574 --> 00:01:54,558
Read every field of a commit object.

27
00:01:54,898 --> 00:01:58,600
List what git commit writes under
.git, and what it leaves untouched.

28
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:02,263
Compute why a change to any
field gives another commit ID.

29
00:02:02,603 --> 00:02:05,665
State what a commit ID
guarantees, and what it does not.

30
00:02:07,505 --> 00:02:12,027
What a commit is. In one sentence: a commit
is an immutable object that names one

31
00:02:12,067 --> 00:02:16,646
complete snapshot of the project, the commit
or commits it was built on, who wrote

32
00:02:16,686 --> 00:02:20,476
the change and when, who created
the commit and when, and a message.

33
00:02:20,816 --> 00:02:22,949
The manual lists five required fields.

34
00:02:23,289 --> 00:02:27,554
tree: the tree object of the top-level
directory, the full snapshot.

35
00:02:27,594 --> 00:02:28,314
Exactly one.

36
00:02:28,654 --> 00:02:33,239
parent: a commit this one was built on.
None for a root commit, one for an ordinary

37
00:02:33,279 --> 00:02:35,022
commit, two or more for a merge.

38
00:02:35,362 --> 00:02:40,772
author: name, email, time and time-zone
offset of the person who wrote the change.

39
00:02:40,812 --> 00:02:41,038
One.

40
00:02:41,378 --> 00:02:45,152
committer: the same for the person
who created this commit object.

41
00:02:45,192 --> 00:02:45,383
One.

42
00:02:45,723 --> 00:02:48,448
The message: free text
after one empty line.

43
00:02:48,788 --> 00:02:53,693
Quick quiz, three options. Which of these
is stored inside a commit object: the

44
00:02:53,733 --> 00:02:57,700
branch name, the diff, or the
committer's time? Say your answer.

45
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:07,306
The committer's time. A commit holds no
diff, no branch name and no file names.

46
00:03:07,346 --> 00:03:12,294
Optional headers can follow the committer
line: encoding, and gpgsig, a signature.

47
00:03:12,634 --> 00:03:17,645
Inside .git: one object per commit in the
object database, Git's store of objects.

48
00:03:17,685 --> 00:03:22,820
Nothing else describes a commit. There's no
table of commits and no per-branch list.

49
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:26,885
A branch reaches its commits by
following parent IDs from its tip.

50
00:03:27,225 --> 00:03:31,990
How git commit creates a commit. In one
sentence: git commit turns the index into

51
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:36,914
tree objects, wraps the top tree in a new
commit object whose parent is the current

52
00:03:36,954 --> 00:03:40,296
HEAD commit, and moves the
current branch to that object.

53
00:03:40,336 --> 00:03:44,805
It is 🟢 SAFE. The index is the middle
box, and HEAD is the commit you're on.

54
00:03:45,145 --> 00:03:48,716
Six steps, in the order in which
their results depend on each other.

55
00:03:49,056 --> 00:03:52,172
One, content: the index
is the proposed snapshot.

56
00:03:52,512 --> 00:03:57,792
Two, checks and message: the pre-commit
hook runs before the message is obtained,

57
00:03:57,832 --> 00:04:03,309
and the commit-msg hook can reject the
message. A hook is a script Git runs at a set

58
00:04:03,349 --> 00:04:05,345
moment. --no-verify skips both.

59
00:04:05,685 --> 00:04:09,860
Three, trees: Git builds one tree per
directory from the index entries.

60
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:14,489
A tree that the object database already
holds keeps its ID, and nothing new is

61
00:04:14,529 --> 00:04:17,338
stored. git write-tree
performs this step alone.

62
00:04:17,678 --> 00:04:21,521
Four, parent: the commit that HEAD
resolves to becomes the parent.

63
00:04:21,561 --> 00:04:25,992
A first commit has none. A merge in
progress adds the commits in MERGE_HEAD.

64
00:04:26,332 --> 00:04:31,223
Five, the commit object: tree ID, parent
IDs, author, committer and message are

65
00:04:31,263 --> 00:04:34,281
written as one object. Its
hash is the commit ID.

66
00:04:34,621 --> 00:04:39,793
Six, refs: the ref that HEAD names is set
to the new ID, and the reflogs of HEAD and

67
00:04:39,833 --> 00:04:44,880
of the branch each gain a line. A reflog
is Git's local journal of where a ref has

68
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:45,377
pointed.

69
00:04:45,717 --> 00:04:50,714
The state table: the working tree is unchanged,
the current branch ref is set to the

70
00:04:50,754 --> 00:04:53,832
new commit, and the remote
and GitHub are unchanged.

71
00:04:53,872 --> 00:04:58,569
In detached HEAD, HEAD itself is set to
the new commit ID, and no branch moves.

72
00:04:58,909 --> 00:05:03,298
Nothing in a commit says which branch it
is on. It goes wherever HEAD pointed when

73
00:05:03,338 --> 00:05:04,378
you ran the command.

74
00:05:04,718 --> 00:05:09,936
The commit ID. In one sentence: a commit ID
is the hash of the commit object, so one

75
00:05:09,976 --> 00:05:15,069
ID names the snapshot, the metadata and,
through the parent IDs, the whole history

76
00:05:15,109 --> 00:05:16,196
behind the commit.

77
00:05:16,536 --> 00:05:22,197
Precisely: Git hashes the bytes commit,
a space, the size, one NUL byte, and the

78
00:05:22,237 --> 00:05:28,040
object content. With the default object
format the hash is SHA-1 and the ID has 40

79
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:31,461
hexadecimal digits. A
SHA-256 repository has 64.

80
00:05:31,801 --> 00:05:35,569
Two properties follow. Same
bytes, same ID, on any machine.

81
00:05:35,609 --> 00:05:40,927
The replays in this course pin the identity
and the clock, which is the only reason

82
00:05:40,967 --> 00:05:43,251
your IDs can equal the printed ones.

83
00:05:43,591 --> 00:05:48,810
And any change, new ID. The parent line
contains the parent's ID, so an ID depends

84
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:54,068
on every ancestor. Nobody can alter an old
commit without changing the ID of every

85
00:05:54,108 --> 00:05:55,094
commit after it.

86
00:05:55,434 --> 00:06:00,427
What the ID does not guarantee. It says
nothing about who really wrote the commit.

87
00:06:00,467 --> 00:06:05,582
Author and committer are assertions: --author,
the configuration and the environment

88
00:06:05,622 --> 00:06:10,431
accept any text. Only a verified signature
is evidence of who created a commit.

89
00:06:10,471 --> 00:06:15,341
And an ID names the commit, and nothing
outside it: a run started from a working

90
00:06:15,381 --> 00:06:19,637
tree with uncommitted edits executed
code that no commit ID describes.

91
00:06:19,977 --> 00:06:25,045
Abbreviated IDs. Any unique prefix of at
least four characters names an object.

92
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:30,346
Git prints abbreviations whose length it
computes from the size of the repository,

93
00:06:30,386 --> 00:06:35,324
seven characters in these sandboxes. A
prefix that is unique today can become

94
00:06:35,364 --> 00:06:40,238
ambiguous later, so deploy records and
model cards should store the full ID.

95
00:06:42,078 --> 00:06:46,659
The textbook's analogy: an entry in a
ledger whose page numbers are computed from

96
00:06:46,699 --> 00:06:51,394
what is written on the page. Each entry
points at a full inventory sheet, cites the

97
00:06:51,434 --> 00:06:56,072
page number of the entry before it, and
names who did the work and who entered it.

98
00:06:56,112 --> 00:06:58,925
Change one character, and
the page number changes.

99
00:06:59,265 --> 00:07:04,420
Now apply it to the hook. Somebody rewrote
the entry on Monday: same inventory

100
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:09,216
sheet, same previous page, a new time
of entry. The page number changed.

101
00:07:09,256 --> 00:07:11,880
The inventory didn't. That's Y beside X.

102
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:17,303
The analogy breaks at the binding. A ledger
is one sequence, while an entry in Git

103
00:07:17,343 --> 00:07:21,864
may cite two earlier entries, which
is a merge, or none, which is a root.

104
00:07:23,704 --> 00:07:28,663
The ref points at the commit. The commit
box lists its fields: one tree, no parent,

105
00:07:28,703 --> 00:07:32,516
an author with a time, a committer
with a time, and the message.

106
00:07:32,556 --> 00:07:34,745
The tree arrow leads to the snapshot.

107
00:07:35,085 --> 00:07:38,903
That box is the hook, written out.
We return to it after the demo.

108
00:07:40,743 --> 00:07:44,978
A new repository, evalkit, and its
first commit. git commit 🟢 SAFE.

109
00:07:45,318 --> 00:07:49,567
(root-commit) in the summary line
says that this commit has no parent.

110
00:07:49,607 --> 00:07:50,915
Now the object itself.

111
00:07:51,255 --> 00:07:55,673
git cat-file -p prints the object
as stored. Read every field.

112
00:07:55,713 --> 00:08:00,634
The tree line names the snapshot,
and HEAD^{tree} asks for that tree.

113
00:08:00,674 --> 00:08:06,459
There's no parent line. author and committer
end with a time in seconds since the

114
00:08:06,499 --> 00:08:10,126
first of January 1970, UTC,
and an offset from UTC.

115
00:08:10,166 --> 00:08:13,362
One empty line separates
headers and message.

116
00:08:13,702 --> 00:08:18,210
Now a second commit, watched from the
plumbing side, the low-level commands.

117
00:08:18,250 --> 00:08:22,280
The script stages an edit and runs
git write-tree before committing.

118
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:27,308
Predict two things: which ID will appear on
the new commit's tree line, and which on

119
00:08:27,348 --> 00:08:29,941
its parent line. Say it
out loud. I'll wait.

120
00:08:34,781 --> 00:08:40,491
git write-tree printed 27d56e4, and the new
commit's tree line carries the same ID:

121
00:08:40,531 --> 00:08:46,240
the commit recorded the index. git rev-parse
HEAD printed 51d62b3, which is now the

122
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:47,072
parent line.

123
00:08:47,411 --> 00:08:52,928
.git/HEAD still holds the same text. The
commit moved the branch, and HEAD follows

124
00:08:52,968 --> 00:08:57,197
only because it names the branch.
Both reflogs gained an entry.

125
00:09:03,337 --> 00:09:08,848
Two reading commands. --format=fuller
shows both identities and both dates.

126
00:09:08,888 --> 00:09:14,991
--format=raw --no-patch shows the same
headers as the stored object, with the ID in

127
00:09:15,031 --> 00:09:17,285
front and the message indented.

128
00:09:17,625 --> 00:09:22,267
A second sandbox with a similar edit.
This time the script compares the object

129
00:09:22,307 --> 00:09:27,188
database before and after each command.
Predict: which objects does git add write,

130
00:09:27,228 --> 00:09:29,109
and which does git commit write?

131
00:09:29,449 --> 00:09:34,710
git add wrote the blob, the file's content.
So the content is in the object database

132
00:09:34,750 --> 00:09:36,287
before any commit exists.

133
00:09:36,627 --> 00:09:41,398
The commit wrote three objects: a tree for
evalkit/, a tree for the top level, and

134
00:09:41,438 --> 00:09:46,091
the commit. It wrote no blob, and the new
top-level tree points at the README.md

135
00:09:46,131 --> 00:09:48,438
blob that the first commit already uses.

136
00:09:48,778 --> 00:09:53,214
Now every file under .git that
is new or changed by the commit.

137
00:09:53,254 --> 00:09:56,909
Predict whether .git/HEAD
is in the list. Yes or no?

138
00:09:56,949 --> 00:09:58,046
Say it out loud.

139
00:10:02,886 --> 00:10:08,188
No. Eight files: three objects, the branch
ref, two reflogs, the index, with the

140
00:10:08,228 --> 00:10:13,597
same entries and refreshed bookkeeping,
and COMMIT_EDITMSG with the last message.

141
00:10:13,637 --> 00:10:18,271
.git/HEAD is not in the list. If you
said yes, you're in good company.

142
00:10:18,611 --> 00:10:21,507
Compute an ID by hand,
then let Git confirm it.

143
00:10:21,847 --> 00:10:26,415
Three lines print the same ID,
1f9c5d8. First git rev-parse HEAD.

144
00:10:26,455 --> 00:10:31,448
Then shasum over the word commit, the
size, a NUL byte and the content.

145
00:10:31,488 --> 00:10:37,120
Then git hash-object -t commit --stdin,
which hashes the text the way git commit

146
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:39,601
does. There's no secret ingredient.

147
00:10:39,941 --> 00:10:45,272
Rebuild the same commit from its parts with
git commit-tree 🟢 SAFE, which adds one

148
00:10:45,312 --> 00:10:49,726
commit object and moves no ref.
Identical inputs gave 1f9c5d8 again.

149
00:10:50,066 --> 00:10:54,589
Now change one input at a time.
Try it on paper, thirty seconds.

150
00:10:54,629 --> 00:11:00,435
Seven changes are coming: message, author
date, committer date, time zone, parent,

151
00:11:00,475 --> 00:11:04,641
tree, email. For each, write
yes or no: will the ID change?

152
00:11:04,681 --> 00:11:06,923
Pause me, and write your answer.

153
00:11:11,763 --> 00:11:16,835
Seven times yes. Seven changes, seven new
IDs. One more character in the message.

154
00:11:16,875 --> 00:11:21,504
One second on the author date. One
second on the committer date is enough.

155
00:11:21,544 --> 00:11:26,616
The same instant with another offset is a
different commit, because the offset is

156
00:11:26,656 --> 00:11:30,466
part of the text. So is a capital
letter in an email address.

157
00:11:30,806 --> 00:11:36,210
So here's your answer for the CTO. X and
Y are two commits with the same tree, and

158
00:11:36,250 --> 00:11:41,521
comparing the two tree IDs proves it. Then
the branch moves back to X, or Y gets

159
00:11:41,561 --> 00:11:42,119
approved.

160
00:11:43,959 --> 00:11:45,607
Five mistakes to watch for.

161
00:11:45,947 --> 00:11:49,526
Saying "I only fixed the message,
the code is the same commit".

162
00:11:49,566 --> 00:11:54,352
Root cause: the message is part of the commit
object, and the ID is the hash of that

163
00:11:54,392 --> 00:11:57,282
object; it is the same tree
and a different commit.

164
00:11:57,622 --> 00:12:02,367
Concluding from two different
IDs that the code differs.

165
00:12:02,407 --> 00:12:09,203
Root cause: the ID covers metadata too;
compare X^{tree} and Y^{tree} to compare

166
00:12:09,243 --> 00:12:09,887
content.

167
00:12:10,227 --> 00:12:15,763
Expecting .git/HEAD to change on a commit.
Root cause: the commit sets the ref that

168
00:12:15,803 --> 00:12:19,256
HEAD names; HEAD itself
still holds the branch name.

169
00:12:19,596 --> 00:12:24,315
Committing on the wrong branch, or on
none. Root cause: nothing in a commit says

170
00:12:24,355 --> 00:12:29,312
which branch it is on; it goes wherever HEAD
pointed, so check git status before you

171
00:12:29,352 --> 00:12:30,382
commit, not after.

172
00:12:30,722 --> 00:12:35,971
Storing abbreviated IDs in deploy records.
Root cause: a prefix that is unique today

173
00:12:36,011 --> 00:12:37,671
can become ambiguous later.

174
00:12:39,511 --> 00:12:44,429
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation run is
tracked with the commit ID of the code.

175
00:12:44,469 --> 00:12:49,203
The textbook's warning: a commit ID in
a deploy record or an evaluation report

176
00:12:49,243 --> 00:12:52,875
identifies the code exactly,
and nothing outside the commit.

177
00:12:52,915 --> 00:12:57,649
A run started from a working tree with
uncommitted edits executed code that no

178
00:12:57,689 --> 00:13:02,362
commit ID describes, and trackers may
not notice: the textbook cites MLflow's

179
00:13:02,402 --> 00:13:07,075
classic run context, which records the
commit and doesn't inspect uncommitted

180
00:13:07,115 --> 00:13:07,565
changes.

181
00:13:07,905 --> 00:13:13,083
The remedy it gives: record git rev-parse
HEAD together with git status --porcelain,

182
00:13:13,123 --> 00:13:16,437
or refuse a tracked run when
that output is not empty.

183
00:13:16,777 --> 00:13:21,592
And for deployments, the prevention line
of the root-cause box: gate deploys on the

184
00:13:21,632 --> 00:13:23,581
approved ID, not on a branch name.

185
00:13:25,421 --> 00:13:32,235
Your turn. Do Exercise 3.1, Level 1,
"read a commit field by field", in

186
00:13:32,275 --> 00:13:35,421
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

187
00:13:35,761 --> 00:13:40,091
Before you print the object, write down
the fields you expect, in order, and for

188
00:13:40,131 --> 00:13:44,515
each one say whether it would differ if
the same change were committed one minute

189
00:13:44,555 --> 00:13:44,843
later.

190
00:13:46,683 --> 00:13:51,479
Q8: "What exactly does git commit write
inside .git, and what does it leave

191
00:13:51,519 --> 00:13:52,188
untouched?"

192
00:13:54,528 --> 00:13:59,311
Answer out loud. A strong answer lists
objects, refs and other files separately,

193
00:13:59,351 --> 00:14:04,195
says which objects are not written because
they already exist, and names the file

194
00:14:04,235 --> 00:14:09,259
that people expect to change and that doesn't.
Mention the case in which the list is

195
00:14:09,299 --> 00:14:11,610
different because no branch is current.

196
00:14:13,450 --> 00:14:18,437
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say, in your own words: a commit object

197
00:14:18,477 --> 00:14:23,587
has a tree, zero or more parents, an author,
a committer and a message, and holds no

198
00:14:23,627 --> 00:14:25,977
diff, no branch name and no file names.

199
00:14:26,317 --> 00:14:31,262
git commit writes trees from the index,
writes the commit with HEAD's commit as

200
00:14:31,302 --> 00:14:35,048
parent, moves the current branch
and appends to two reflogs.

201
00:14:35,088 --> 00:14:38,013
It doesn't touch .git/HEAD
or the working tree.

202
00:14:38,353 --> 00:14:43,319
The commit ID is a hash over all of the
object, parents included, so it fixes the

203
00:14:43,359 --> 00:14:47,645
whole history behind it, and any
change to any field gives another ID.

204
00:14:47,685 --> 00:14:52,157
It guarantees content and history. It
doesn't prove who wrote the commit.

205
00:14:53,997 --> 00:14:57,622
Read sections 6.1 to 6.4 of Chapter 6.

206
00:14:57,962 --> 00:15:03,552
Do Lab 3.2, "Change only the committer
date and watch the ID change", in

207
00:15:03,592 --> 00:15:05,585
lab-manual/m03-commits.md.

208
00:15:05,925 --> 00:15:13,209
Challenge: Exercise 3.7, Level 3, "one
ahead, one behind, and nobody else pushed",

209
00:15:13,249 --> 00:15:16,425
in exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

210
00:15:16,765 --> 00:15:20,619
Today you read a commit byte by
byte and computed its ID by hand.

211
